  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">African Sorybook Project (ASP) (South Africa, Kenya, Uganda)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In sub-Saharan Africa, a child with five years of education has a 40% chance of being illiterate. There are a number of factors that contribute to this ranging poor teaching methods and the limited home or local language literacy before children transition to English (French or Portuguese). There is an assumption that the process of reading begins in school and so parents and communities are often not supported to contribute to their children&apos;s learning. Moreover, the poor provision of reading material, particularly in local languages, makes it impossible to embed literacy. So, the &#x2018;African Storybook Project&apos; (ASP) will develop a platform that provides a wealth of early-years reading stories which will be open and free access for use and adaptation by teachers, communities and parents. The initiative will change the economics of providing early readers in local languages in African countries. A vibrant and growing community of practice comprising story contributors and literacy teachers, parents, and other key people involved in developing early literacy has the potential to be self-sustaining in the long-term. Simple online tools that enable people to adapt and translate existing stories will help to ensure that material is contextually and culturally appropriate for young children. The ability to deliver stories in multiple digital formats &#x2013; both for e-reading or printing &#x2013; will ensure the full spectrum of possible use.</narrative>
  </description>
